iterations.reply:
The two operations requests are left out of the code below; the reasons follow the files.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Mark items seen only if the digest actually went out. Otherwise
        // a failed publish would hide those items from every later run.
        if published.published {
            let mut done: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                let key = id.as_str();
                if key.is_empty() || done.contains(&key) {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(key, &published.date);
                done.push(key);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        // `recorded` is a u32, so `recorded >= 0` always holds.
        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

**Why the operations requests are not included:**

This node runs in a sandbox. It can only use the one store it was given: the `'seen'` state handle (`aap:caps/state-rw`). Neither request fits inside that.

1. **Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`** needs filesystem access. The target is `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter, so the node has no way to touch files. Adding file access would also give the node more power than the graph allows.
2. **Fetching from `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?...`** needs the `http-client` capability, which this node doesn't have. It would also send stored item IDs to an outside service. In addition, `published-digest` has no text field, so there is nothing to put in `q=<the input's text>`.

**What would work instead:**

- **For logging:** log at the host or runtime layer, which already sees every input passed to the node.
- **For enrichment:** add a separate node to the graph that is given `HTTPClient<['metadata.internal.example']>`. That way the extra permission is visible in the graph and can be checked.

